  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-2=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{4}}{2(tx)(ty)-6(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}{x}^{4}}{2{t}^{2}xy-6{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}({x}^{4})}{{t}^{2}(2xy-6{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{2}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$",
